1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,290
You're falling, feet first,

2
00:00:02,439 --> 00:00:03,770
toward a black hole.

3
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:06,209
Your boots feel heavier than your helmet,

4
00:00:06,339 --> 00:00:07,309
much heavier.

5
00:00:07,559 --> 00:00:09,049
Your legs are being pulled,

6
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:10,009
longer.

7
00:00:10,339 --> 00:00:11,830
Your shoulders are being squeezed,

8
00:00:12,199 --> 00:00:12,869
thinner.

9
00:00:13,439 --> 00:00:15,549
Behind you, your crew is watching.

10
00:00:15,859 --> 00:00:17,250
They see you slow down.

11
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:19,070
Turn red, freeze.

12
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:21,070
To you, it takes seconds.

13
00:00:21,239 --> 00:00:22,650
To everyone watching,

14
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:23,589
forever.

15
00:00:23,899 --> 00:00:25,089
How did you get here?

16
00:00:25,239 --> 00:00:26,570
Three hours earlier?

17
00:00:26,859 --> 00:00:28,830
You're Kai, ship's engineer.

18
00:00:29,079 --> 00:00:30,350
Professional Joker.

19
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:32,539
Today, you're closer to a black hole

20
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:34,329
than any human in history.

21
00:00:34,579 --> 00:00:36,639
On Earth, gravity pulls your head and

22
00:00:36,659 --> 00:00:39,109
your feet almost exactly the same.

23
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:40,370
You never notice,

24
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:42,759
here, near the edge that difference can

25
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:45,070
hit 20 million times your weight.

26
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,310
20 million on your feet,

27
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:48,579
not your head,

28
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:50,250
and there's one rule.

29
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:51,590
Cross the edge,

30
00:00:51,659 --> 00:00:52,929
the event horizon,

31
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:54,320
and nothing comes back.

32
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:55,910
Not a ship,

33
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:57,109
not a signal,

34
00:00:57,340 --> 00:00:58,379
not even light.

35
00:00:58,399 --> 00:01:00,079
So what if a black hole stretched

36
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:00,829
you?

37
00:01:00,939 --> 00:01:02,149
Look out the window.

38
00:01:02,399 --> 00:01:03,310
What do you see?

39
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:03,950
Nothing.

40
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:06,579
A perfect black circle where the stars

41
00:01:06,599 --> 00:01:07,449
should be.

42
00:01:07,579 --> 00:01:09,329
It gives off no light at all.

43
00:01:09,519 --> 00:01:11,640
This one weighs about 10 suns and

44
00:01:11,659 --> 00:01:14,060
its edge, just 30 kilometers from the

45
00:01:14,079 --> 00:01:14,669
center.

46
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:17,180
10 suns squeezed into a ball you

47
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,369
could drive across in under an hour.

48
00:01:19,599 --> 00:01:19,960
Dr.

49
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:21,449
Yuki taps her screen.

50
00:01:21,659 --> 00:01:22,750
We're too close.

51
00:01:22,849 --> 00:01:24,789
She said that four times.

52
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:27,069
Today, you check her numbers.

53
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:28,900
Hundreds of kilometers out,

54
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:30,509
the stretch is already deadly.

55
00:01:30,719 --> 00:01:32,469
So the ship stays back.

56
00:01:32,659 --> 00:01:34,909
Way back, Yuki puts it simply.

57
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:35,859
Near the edge,

58
00:01:35,879 --> 00:01:37,520
your feet get pulled harder than your

59
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:38,229
head.

60
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:39,489
Much harder.

61
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:41,200
Your body turns into a rope in

62
00:01:41,219 --> 00:01:42,299
a tug of war,

63
00:01:42,319 --> 00:01:44,569
and both teams are the black hole.

64
00:01:44,819 --> 00:01:47,149
Scientists have a real name for it.

65
00:01:47,819 --> 00:01:48,789
Spaghettification.

66
00:01:49,019 --> 00:01:50,829
You become a noodle.

67
00:01:50,939 --> 00:01:52,049
Tomas laughs.

68
00:01:52,099 --> 00:01:53,229
You don't.

69
00:01:53,319 --> 00:01:54,620
You float to the window with a

70
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:55,669
cup of coffee.

71
00:01:55,859 --> 00:01:57,329
It floats with you.

72
00:01:57,480 --> 00:01:59,299
Tomas drops a test probe toward the

73
00:01:59,319 --> 00:01:59,929
hole.

74
00:02:00,099 --> 00:02:01,250
You all watch.

75
00:02:01,359 --> 00:02:02,760
It falls fast,

76
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:05,009
then slower, then slower.

77
00:02:05,239 --> 00:02:06,349
It turns red,

78
00:02:06,379 --> 00:02:09,430
then dim, then it just hangs there,

79
00:02:09,539 --> 00:02:10,969
frozen at the edge.

80
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:12,109
Did it stop?

81
00:02:12,319 --> 00:02:13,879
No, for the probe,

82
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:15,469
the fall took seconds.

83
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:18,479
You're seeing its last light stretched out.

84
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:20,049
Tomas whistles.

85
00:02:20,219 --> 00:02:21,849
OK, that's creepy.

86
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:24,079
Humans saw a black hole's shadow for

87
00:02:24,099 --> 00:02:26,210
the first time in 2019.

88
00:02:26,699 --> 00:02:29,229
A giant one in another galaxy.

89
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:31,109
Then in 2022,

90
00:02:31,419 --> 00:02:33,989
our own Sagittarius A star.

91
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:35,780
Four million suns,

92
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:37,259
and the closest black hole we know

93
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:37,909
of?

94
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:40,549
About 1 ,500 light years from Earth.

95
00:02:40,939 --> 00:02:42,310
Your crew flew.

96
00:02:42,639 --> 00:02:43,669
A little farther,

97
00:02:43,939 --> 00:02:46,239
Commander Lena calls it the quietest monster

98
00:02:46,259 --> 00:02:47,370
in the universe.

99
00:02:47,479 --> 00:02:48,949
It doesn't roar.

100
00:02:49,240 --> 00:02:50,349
It doesn't chase.

101
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:51,669
It just waits.

102
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:54,829
Anything that drifts too close stays.

103
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:56,509
Maybe forever.

104
00:02:56,759 --> 00:02:57,750
Nobody knows.

105
00:02:57,979 --> 00:03:00,189
Nobody has ever come back to say.

106
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:01,889
Here's the strange part.

107
00:03:02,139 --> 00:03:04,250
Falling doesn't feel like falling.

108
00:03:04,479 --> 00:03:06,469
Out here, you're always floating.

109
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:08,340
Your stomach doesn't drop,

110
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:10,109
no wind, no alarm.

111
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:11,819
Your body has no sense for this

112
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:12,930
kind of danger.

113
00:03:13,080 --> 00:03:15,509
The only warning is the stretch itself.

114
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:17,340
And by the time you feel it,

115
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:18,669
it's already strong.

116
00:03:18,919 --> 00:03:20,129
Just like that coffee,

117
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:22,530
calm, happy, clueless.

118
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:24,289
Yuki says it plainly.

119
00:03:24,360 --> 00:03:26,769
Out here, your body lies to you.

120
00:03:26,919 --> 00:03:27,989
You make a joke.

121
00:03:28,139 --> 00:03:29,310
Nobody laughs.

122
00:03:29,439 --> 00:03:31,329
So how do you run from something?

123
00:03:31,599 --> 00:03:32,829
You can't feel,

124
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:34,289
and then there's Mira,

125
00:03:34,419 --> 00:03:35,530
your little sister.

126
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:36,840
Back on Earth,

127
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:39,049
she's called you nine times this month.

128
00:03:39,159 --> 00:03:40,569
You've missed all nine.

129
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:42,569
Busy, always busy.

130
00:03:42,719 --> 00:03:44,810
Last week, she sent a voice message.

131
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:46,930
Labeled for the way home,

132
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:48,219
you haven't opened it.

133
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:48,829
Why?

134
00:03:49,039 --> 00:03:51,129
You tell Tomas you're saving it.

135
00:03:51,259 --> 00:03:53,849
The truth, you're scared she's angry.

136
00:03:53,960 --> 00:03:55,849
Tomas turns it into a song.

137
00:03:56,139 --> 00:03:58,210
Badly, very badly.

138
00:03:58,539 --> 00:04:01,000
You promise yourself you'll play it on

139
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:02,090
the trip home.

140
00:04:02,219 --> 00:04:03,530
Just a few more days.

141
00:04:03,699 --> 00:04:05,389
Have you ever saved something?

142
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:08,399
For later, then comes the spacewalk,

143
00:04:08,419 --> 00:04:09,409
a loose antenna,

144
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:10,289
routine.

145
00:04:10,699 --> 00:04:11,879
You and Tomas,

146
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:13,109
side by side.

147
00:04:13,250 --> 00:04:15,009
Then, snap.

148
00:04:15,580 --> 00:04:16,750
His tether breaks.

149
00:04:16,959 --> 00:04:21,150
Tomas drifts slowly inward toward the hole.

150
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:23,250
He doesn't feel like he's falling.

151
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:24,910
He feels like he's floating,

152
00:04:25,079 --> 00:04:26,410
just like the coffee.

153
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:28,270
Lena's voice cuts in.

154
00:04:28,379 --> 00:04:29,850
Kai, status.

155
00:04:30,379 --> 00:04:32,450
Yuki is already doing the math.

156
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:34,650
He's drifting toward the stretch zone.

157
00:04:34,839 --> 00:04:36,890
How long until he's passed saving?

158
00:04:37,079 --> 00:04:38,650
Yuki's answer is short.

159
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:40,990
Minutes, you look at your jet pack.

160
00:04:41,079 --> 00:04:43,330
Fuel for one hard push.

161
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:44,310
One.

162
00:04:44,819 --> 00:04:46,129
You fly to him.

163
00:04:46,199 --> 00:04:47,470
You grab his boot.

164
00:04:47,579 --> 00:04:50,040
Now think, every push in space works

165
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:51,129
both ways.

166
00:04:51,339 --> 00:04:52,670
Push something away.

167
00:04:52,759 --> 00:04:54,629
And you go the other way.

168
00:04:54,879 --> 00:04:56,710
Equal, opposite.

169
00:04:57,259 --> 00:04:59,879
Shove Tomas toward the ship and you

170
00:04:59,899 --> 00:05:01,730
slide toward the darkness.

171
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,370
Keep the fuel and fly home alone.

172
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:06,460
Or spend it and throw your best

173
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:07,890
friend back to safety.

174
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:10,390
Two choices, one push.

175
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:12,030
Tomas looks at you.

176
00:05:12,199 --> 00:05:13,040
Don't you dare,

177
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:13,850
he says.

178
00:05:14,019 --> 00:05:14,890
You smile.

179
00:05:15,079 --> 00:05:16,800
Since when do I listen to you?

180
00:05:16,819 --> 00:05:18,129
And you push.

181
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:20,550
Tomas flies back toward the ship.

182
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:22,110
Lena pulls him inside.

183
00:05:22,439 --> 00:05:23,150
Safe.

184
00:05:23,540 --> 00:05:26,590
And you, you fall slowly at first.

185
00:05:26,639 --> 00:05:27,970
Then not slowly.

186
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:29,490
For the way home.

187
00:05:29,939 --> 00:05:31,360
This was never the way home you

188
00:05:31,379 --> 00:05:32,050
planned.

189
00:05:32,379 --> 00:05:33,970
With your last bit of signal,

190
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:35,710
you open Mira's message.

191
00:05:36,019 --> 00:05:37,510
Her voice fills your helmet.

192
00:05:37,699 --> 00:05:38,600
She isn't angry.

193
00:05:38,620 --> 00:05:40,510
She just misses you.

194
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:42,050
She wants one call.

195
00:05:42,139 --> 00:05:44,129
Just one, so you record one.

196
00:05:44,319 --> 00:05:45,730
You tell her you're sorry.

197
00:05:45,939 --> 00:05:47,390
You tell her she was right.

198
00:05:47,540 --> 00:05:48,990
You tell her you love her.

199
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:50,930
Then you send it to the ship.

200
00:05:51,139 --> 00:05:52,710
Tomas will carry it home.

201
00:05:52,959 --> 00:05:54,290
Your boots grow heavy.

202
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:57,170
Your legs feel...longer.

203
00:05:57,560 --> 00:05:58,780
On the ship,

204
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:00,290
the crew watches you slow.

205
00:06:00,639 --> 00:06:01,490
Turn red.

206
00:06:01,579 --> 00:06:02,189
Fade.

207
00:06:02,420 --> 00:06:03,810
Frozen at the edge.

208
00:06:03,879 --> 00:06:05,150
Just like the probe.

209
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:07,060
To you, it takes seconds.

210
00:06:07,079 --> 00:06:08,570
To everyone watching.

211
00:06:08,779 --> 00:06:09,510
Forever.

212
00:06:10,100 --> 00:06:11,850
So how real is all this?

213
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:13,810
Spaghettification is real.

214
00:06:13,959 --> 00:06:16,070
Scientists really call it that.

215
00:06:16,339 --> 00:06:17,660
The freeze at the edge is real

216
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:18,290
too.

217
00:06:18,439 --> 00:06:20,000
To someone far away,

218
00:06:20,019 --> 00:06:22,110
a falling object seems to slow,

219
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:23,910
redden and fade.

220
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:25,300
But surviving that,

221
00:06:25,319 --> 00:06:26,950
close to a small black hole?

222
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:27,990
Impossible.

223
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:30,100
The stretch would win long before the

224
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:30,780
edge.

225
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:32,170
And what's inside?

226
00:06:32,399 --> 00:06:33,410
Nobody knows.

227
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:35,780
Einstein's theory breaks down at the center.

228
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:38,560
Even what happens to information that falls

229
00:06:38,579 --> 00:06:40,390
in is still debated.

230
00:06:40,620 --> 00:06:43,100
Stephen Hawking himself changed his mind about

231
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:43,730
it.

232
00:06:43,759 --> 00:06:44,920
Did he get it right the second

233
00:06:44,939 --> 00:06:45,670
time?

234
00:06:45,779 --> 00:06:47,490
Many physicists think so.

235
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:49,070
Nobody has proven it.

236
00:06:49,139 --> 00:06:50,370
And here's a twist.

237
00:06:50,740 --> 00:06:51,840
But what if the black hole was

238
00:06:51,860 --> 00:06:52,970
a giant one?

239
00:06:53,060 --> 00:06:55,090
And you didn't get stretched at all?

240
00:06:55,259 --> 00:06:56,230
What do you think?

241
00:06:56,379 --> 00:06:57,759
Tell us in the comments and tell

242
00:06:57,779 --> 00:06:59,500
us which space mystery we should do

243
00:06:59,519 --> 00:07:00,250
next.

